-- Passage rg9-main-idea-summary-p01
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('f8ffdc5d-ec43-5b96-b88d-2d24c3003147', 'rg9-main-idea-summary-p01', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g9-main-idea-summary'), null,
        '9-12', $q$When Ice Came from Ponds$q$, $q$Long before electric freezers, people in the fictional river town of Merrow Falls kept their food cold with ice cut from frozen ponds. Every January, when the ice on Fenwick Pond grew thicker than a foot, the town turned the pond into a busy workplace.

The harvest followed careful steps. First, workers cleared the snow so the ice would freeze evenly. Then a horse pulled a sharp plow that scored the surface into a grid of long lines. Workers with hand saws cut along the lines and floated the heavy blocks down a channel of open water to a ramp. From there, the blocks were hauled into a tall wooden icehouse beside the pond.

The icehouse was the secret to making winter last. Its thick walls were packed with sawdust, and every layer of blocks was covered with more sawdust or straw. Because these materials slowed the flow of heat, much of the ice stayed frozen until late summer. Families bought blocks from a delivery wagon and placed them in wooden iceboxes in their kitchens.

The work was cold and hard, but it gave the town steady winter jobs. According to records kept by the Merrow Falls Historical Society, more than eighty people worked the harvest in a good year. Local dairies depended on the ice to keep milk fresh, and the town's butcher used it to store meat through the hot months.

The pond harvest ended in the 1930s, when electric refrigerators became affordable and factories began making ice year round. Today the old icehouse is a small museum, where visitors can lift a pair of iron ice tongs and imagine how a frozen pond once kept a whole town supplied through the summer.$q$, 'draft')
